  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". Consider these two reply texts. Reply A: \"Some prose.\\n```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n{\\\"b\\\":2}\\n```\\nLater prose with object: {\\\"outside\\\": \\\"same\\\"}\". Reply B: \"Some prose.\\n```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n{\\\"c\\\":3}\\n```\\nLater prose with object: {\\\"outside\\\": \\\"same\\\"}\". For Reply A, the rule requires recover_json_object to return the dictionary {\\\"b\\\":2}, because the last object inside the code fence is {\\\"b\\\":2}. For Reply B, the rule requires recover_json_object to return the dictionary {\\\"c\\\":3}, because the last object inside the code fence is {\\\"c\\\":3}. The function, however, returns the same answer for both: it returns {\\\"outside\\\": \\\"same\\\"}, because the fenced candidate (the entire fence content) fails to parse as a single JSON object, so the function falls back to the top-level pool and picks the last top-level object in the whole text, which is the later object outside the fence. This conjecture would be false if recover_json_object returned {\\\"b\\\":2} for Reply A and {\\\"c\\\":3} for Reply B, or if the documented rule were interpreted to allow the later top-level object outside the fence to take precedence when the fenced content is not a single parseable object."
